+NOTES = r"""
+Paper grounding (Step 0).  Section 2 defines an isogeny as a nonconstant
+algebraic group map with finite kernel.  Proposition "Horizontal and vertical
+isogenies" and the isogeny-graph definition in Section 6 identify graph edges
+with prime-degree isogenies.  Section 7's theorem "Supersingular graphs are
+Ramanujan" says that, for distinct primes p and l, the supersingular
+l-isogeny graph over the algebraic closure is connected, (l+1)-regular and
+Ramanujan.  We use l=2.  Section 8 defines the Isogeny path problem and states
+that only algorithms exponential in log(#E) are known in general; it gives the
+birthday/meet-in-the-middle strategy.  The CGL subsection defines a hash as a
+non-backtracking walk.  Section 9 states more precisely that a hidden walk of
+degree l^e is recovered by meet-in-the-middle in O(l^(e/2)) time and storage,
+and observes that a non-backtracking l-walk has a cyclic kernel of order l^e.
+
